 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered over self-hosted WebSockets: the server holds a persistent connection to each browser and pushes new revisions as they commit, without a third-party realtime service. Self-hosted sockets deliver fast updates; subsecond latency is useful but not required.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around this choice. The change store retains replayable revisions and publishes them reliably to the delivery layer. Delivery manages socket sessions and fans committed revisions out to subscribed browsers. Browsers hold a subscription, detect disconnection, and catch up from their last-seen revision on reconnect. The project owns connection capacity and reconnect handling rather than paying for and coupling to a realtime vendor.
 
